WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.665
Paris 1746. 

00:00:02.665 --> 00:00:05.518
200 monks have been arranged into&nbsp;a line

00:00:05.518 --> 00:00:07.442
each connected by a wire. 

00:00:07.442 --> 00:00:08.695
At the end of the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.695 --> 00:00:11.045
French scientist Jean-Antoine Nollet &nbsp;

00:00:11.045 --> 00:00:14.080
is&nbsp;about to connect the final monk to a  leyden jar,&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.970
a primitive battery that 
stored high voltage&nbsp;static electricity. 

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.855
Without warning, he connects&nbsp;the battery

00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:21.441
and to everyone's surprise

00:00:21.441 --> 00:00:26.079
all 200&nbsp;monks are shocked at the exact same time.

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:26.995
 As it&nbsp;turned out, 

00:00:26.995 --> 00:00:29.466
electricity didn't just travel&nbsp;fast

00:00:29.466 --> 00:00:32.220
it appeared to be instantaneous. 

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.515
This&nbsp;was a huge breakthrough. 

00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:37.018
If electricity could&nbsp;travel long distances 

00:00:37.018 --> 00:00:39.120
at instantaneous speeds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.088
could it be used to deliver 
messages across the&nbsp;country,

00:00:42.088 --> 00:00:44.584
 or perhaps around the world? 

00:00:44.584 --> 00:00:45.440
This question

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.953
started &nbsp;one of the most 
innovative periods&nbsp;in human history

00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:51.710
and eventually led to the&nbsp;internet. 

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.570
No, not that internet. 

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.899
In the 1800s,&nbsp;

00:00:55.899 --> 00:00:59.109
there were no computers, phones, or even light&nbsp;bulbs,

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:01.232
but there was an internet.

00:01:01.232 --> 00:01:04.263
A worldwide&nbsp;web of cables crossing oceans 

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:07.719
and connecting&nbsp;countries with instant communication. 

00:01:07.719 --> 00:01:10.960
People&nbsp;did business, fell in love, and got scammed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.309
just like today. 

00:01:12.309 --> 00:01:14.726
But how did we get there? 

00:01:14.726 --> 00:01:16.210
In the&nbsp;early 1700s, 

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.960
long-distance communication was slow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.982
A messenger on a horse could take up to a day&nbsp;

00:01:21.982 --> 00:01:24.640
just to deliver your letter to the next town.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.071
Sending a letter from Europe to America?

00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:30.559
Don't&nbsp;expect a reply for another 6 months. 

00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:33.360
It had&nbsp;been this way for all of human existence,&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.035
but that was about to change. 

00:01:36.035 --> 00:01:37.840
In 1792,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.783
French inventor Claude Chappe

00:01:39.783 --> 00:01:42.001
set up a series of&nbsp;towers, 

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:44.735
each with a mechanical arm on top. 

00:01:44.735 --> 00:01:48.037
An&nbsp;operator would control 
the arms from inside the&nbsp;tower, 

00:01:48.037 --> 00:01:50.080
and by rotating them in different ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.745
a total of 92 unique signals could be created. 

00:01:53.745 --> 00:01:55.547
A&nbsp;few miles down the line, 

00:01:55.547 --> 00:01:58.486
the operator in the next&nbsp;tower 
would look through a telescope&nbsp;

00:01:58.486 --> 00:02:00.237
and copy the&nbsp;signal. 

00:02:00.237 --> 00:02:02.320
This process would continue down the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.848
quickly spreading the signal 
over several miles&nbsp;in just a matter of seconds.

00:02:06.848 --> 00:02:07.840
 For each word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.706
two signals would be sent. 

00:02:09.706 --> 00:02:13.650
The first would tell&nbsp;the operator 
which page number to look at in the&nbsp;code book. 

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:18.210
The second signal would correspond&nbsp;
to a specific word on that page. &nbsp;

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.360
Word by word,&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.022
the message would be sent down the line of towers&nbsp;

00:02:22.022 --> 00:02:23.546
and at its destination, &nbsp;

00:02:23.546 --> 00:02:27.536
each signal would be&nbsp;decoded 
back into the full message. 

00:02:27.536 --> 00:02:30.171
Chappe’s system&nbsp;was a huge success 

00:02:30.171 --> 00:02:32.849
and officially became known as the telegraph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:35.714
 His towers started spreading 
all&nbsp;across the country 

00:02:35.714 --> 00:02:38.689
and even played a huge role in&nbsp;the French Revolution,

00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:41.190
alerting the government&nbsp;about enemy invasions &nbsp;

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.007
in a matter of minutes instead of days. &nbsp;

00:02:44.007 --> 00:02:47.279
But Chappe’s optical telegraph&nbsp;had its limits. &nbsp;

00:02:47.279 --> 00:02:49.600
Each tower required employees,&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.324
making the whole system expensive to run. 

00:02:52.324 --> 00:02:55.200
The&nbsp;towers couldn't work at night or in bad weather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.813
and they were only really used 

00:02:56.813 --> 00:03:00.000
by governments to&nbsp;send very simple messages. 

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.208
If there was ever to be a truly fast, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.208 --> 00:03:05.561
long-distance communication&nbsp;system for everyone, 

00:03:05.561 --> 00:03:08.080
it would have to be electric.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.528
Dozens of attempts had already been made, 

00:03:10.528 --> 00:03:13.064
but they&nbsp;all ran into the same problems. 

00:03:13.064 --> 00:03:15.000
The leyden jars that were being used 

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.108
only let out a tiny spark&nbsp;of static electricity 

00:03:18.108 --> 00:03:21.381
which would struggle to&nbsp;travel more than a few miles. &nbsp;

00:03:21.381 --> 00:03:24.017
This was eventually&nbsp;solved in 1800 

00:03:24.017 --> 00:03:28.020
when Allessandro Volta came 
up with&nbsp;the first chemical battery. 

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.812
This could provide a&nbsp;steady flow of electricity 

00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:34.620
capable of traveling&nbsp;hundreds of miles down a wire. &nbsp;

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.784
Here is an early&nbsp;electrical telegraph that used 26 wires, 

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:40.887
one for&nbsp;each letter of the alphabet.

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.319
 At the receiver end,&nbsp;

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:45.827
the electric signal would cause 
a small metallic&nbsp;ball to move, 

00:03:45.827 --> 00:03:47.725
signaling each letter. 

00:03:47.725 --> 00:03:49.824
This was a&nbsp;step in the right direction, 

00:03:49.824 --> 00:03:51.693
but having a wire for each letter &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.693 --> 00:03:56.346
would cost an enormous amount of&nbsp;
money when scaled up to a full-size line. 

00:03:56.346 --> 00:03:59.480
The key&nbsp;was figuring out how to send information

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.919
with just&nbsp;a single wire. 

00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:04.160
In towns all over medieval Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.194
church bells had long been 
used to spread basic&nbsp;information. 

00:04:08.194 --> 00:04:09.344
To tell the time, 

00:04:09.344 --> 00:04:11.074
the bell would ring on the hour &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.074 --> 00:04:14.898
with the amount of rings matching&nbsp;
the number of each hour. 

00:04:14.898 --> 00:04:16.819
Four times for 4:00, 

00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:20.000
five&nbsp;times for 5:00, and so on. 

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.722
When someone in the&nbsp;town died, 

00:04:21.722 --> 00:04:24.800
the bell would do multiple slow rings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.028
three times for a man, and twice for a woman.&nbsp;

00:04:28.028 --> 00:04:31.440
After that, the bell would do multiple short rings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.903
to mark the age of the person who died. 

00:04:33.903 --> 00:04:38.244
If all&nbsp;of this information could be conveyed 
by simple&nbsp;patterns of bell rings,

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:43.391
 perhaps the same could&nbsp;be done 
with electric pulses traveling down a&nbsp;wire.&nbsp;

00:04:43.391 --> 00:04:47.528
Today, we are so used to having 
instant news&nbsp;from every corner of the world. 

00:04:47.528 --> 00:04:51.475
But just because&nbsp;it's fast doesn't make it trustworthy. 

