00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:07.208
In order for the electric&nbsp;telegraph to become a reality, 

00:06:07.208 --> 00:06:10.778
scientists had to&nbsp;figure out 
how to turn pulses of electricity

00:06:10.778 --> 00:06:12.880
 into&nbsp;lines of text. 

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:17.340
That's when American painter 
Samuel Morse entered the race. 

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.213
Instead of sending&nbsp;individual 
signals for each letter of the alphabet, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:26.365
he had the genius idea of using short&nbsp;
and long bursts of electric signals. 

00:06:26.365 --> 00:06:27.892
Much like the church bells, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.892 --> 00:06:32.560
different combinations of these&nbsp;signals 
could be used to send coded messages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.397
He came up with a simple 
tapping device that when pressed

00:06:36.397 --> 00:06:38.400
connected the battery into the circuit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.569
sending a signal down the wire. 

00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:43.045
By simply holding&nbsp;the button down for longer, 

00:06:43.045 --> 00:06:45.270
a longer signal would&nbsp;be sent out. 

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:46.830
At the receiver end,

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.207
the signal would&nbsp;turn on an electromagnet,

00:06:49.207 --> 00:06:52.226
 pulling a small pen onto&nbsp;a moving piece of paper, &nbsp;

00:06:52.226 --> 00:06:55.610
and recording the signal&nbsp;as a series of lines. 

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.792
Together with his engineer&nbsp;Alfred Vail,

00:06:57.792 --> 00:07:04.148
they developed a code assigning each&nbsp;letter in the alphabet a combination of short and long pulses. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.148 --> 00:07:08.930
This eventually became what 
we know&nbsp;today as Morse code. 

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.231
Meanwhile, on the other side&nbsp;of the Atlantic, 

00:07:11.231 --> 00:07:14.211
English inventors William Cooke 
and Charles Wheatstone

00:07:14.211 --> 00:07:17.960
were about to make their own&nbsp;
breakthrough with the electric telegraph. &nbsp;

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.885
Their&nbsp;system used a set of five needles 

00:07:20.885 --> 00:07:25.000
that could be&nbsp;turned to the left 
or right via the press of a&nbsp;button. 

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.335
Each letter was arranged in a diamond&nbsp;pattern,

00:07:27.335 --> 00:07:29.522
and by simply moving two of the needles,&nbsp;

00:07:29.522 --> 00:07:31.443
any letter could be selected. 

00:07:31.443 --> 00:07:33.390
Although it required&nbsp;multiple wires, &nbsp;

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.667
it had one major advantage. 

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:40.000
Unlike&nbsp;the Morse telegraph, it
didn't require a complex&nbsp;code book. 

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.719
The letters were visibly selected each&nbsp;time,

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:46.028
 and anyone could operate 
the machine without&nbsp;training. 

00:07:46.028 --> 00:07:49.354
Both sides set out to get funding for&nbsp;their telegraphs

00:07:49.354 --> 00:07:50.981
And after a lot of persuasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.981 --> 00:07:53.318
Cooke and Wheatstone finally got permission

00:07:53.318 --> 00:07:57.083
 to&nbsp;install their telegraph along 
a short section&nbsp;of railway in England. 

00:07:57.083 --> 00:08:01.760
It was here that the&nbsp;electric telegraph 
would finally become famous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.513
On the 1st of January 1845,&nbsp;

00:08:04.513 --> 00:08:07.049
John Tawell murdered&nbsp;his mistress 

00:08:07.049 --> 00:08:09.849
and escaped by hopping on a train headed for London. &nbsp;

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:11.721
Under normal circumstances,&nbsp;

00:08:11.721 --> 00:08:15.923
this would have been a guaranteed 
way to outrun the spread of news,&nbsp;

00:08:15.923 --> 00:08:19.334
but little did he know about&nbsp;the telegraph. 

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:20.544
After some quick thinking, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.544 --> 00:08:24.039
a police officer sent a description 
of the man through the&nbsp;telegraph

00:08:24.039 --> 00:08:27.725
 and the police in London 
were quickly able to arrest him. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.725 --> 00:08:31.783
The public finally understood&nbsp;
how powerful the telegraph could be. 

00:08:31.783 --> 00:08:32.815
And later that year, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.815 --> 00:08:37.840
the Cooke and Wheatstone telegraph&nbsp;
started being installed all over Britain.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.325
On the other side of the Atlantic, 

00:08:39.325 --> 00:08:41.642
Morse was&nbsp;also finding success, 

00:08:41.642 --> 00:08:46.109
having demonstrated&nbsp;a 40-mile 
transmission from Washington to&nbsp;Baltimore. &nbsp;

00:08:46.109 --> 00:08:48.990
Both countries invested heavily&nbsp;in their telegraphs,&nbsp;

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:50.663
and in just a few years,&nbsp;

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:53.440
thousands of miles had already been installed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.758
connecting the largest cities together. 

00:08:55.758 --> 00:08:57.540
The next&nbsp;step was obvious. 

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.320
Join the two sides together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.395
With the help of American 
businessman Cyrus West Field, 

00:09:03.395 --> 00:09:09.306
the Atlantic Telegraph Company was&nbsp;
set up with the aim to lay a 2,000m long cable&nbsp;

00:09:09.306 --> 00:09:12.240
from Newfoundland all the way to Valencia Island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.249
just off the coast of Ireland.

00:09:14.249 --> 00:09:18.729
 Both countries&nbsp;had mastered 
wiring thousands of lines over&nbsp;land,&nbsp;

00:09:18.729 --> 00:09:21.650
but making a wire that 
could survive&nbsp;the corrosive water &nbsp;

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:24.070
and curious sea life at&nbsp;the bottom of the ocean 

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:26.379
was a completely different&nbsp;challenge. 

00:09:26.379 --> 00:09:28.720
This was the cable they came up with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.020
The core was made from 
seven strands of copper&nbsp;wire 

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:36.480
twisted together and wrapped in 
three layers&nbsp;of rubber to keep it insulated. &nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.115
The cable was then&nbsp;covered in tarred hemp 

00:09:39.115 --> 00:09:43.240
and wrapped in iron wires&nbsp;
to give the cable its protection. &nbsp;

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:46.348
No ship could&nbsp;carry the whole 2,000 mile cable, 

00:09:46.348 --> 00:09:48.768
so it was split&nbsp;into two sections

00:09:48.768 --> 00:09:52.941
and coiled onto enormous barrels&nbsp;
inside two warships. 

00:09:52.941 --> 00:09:55.440
On the 29th of July 1858,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.480
the two ships met in the middle of the Atlantic&nbsp;Ocean, 

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.809
and the two cables were carefully spliced&nbsp;together. &nbsp;

00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:04.877
Both ships then headed out in opposite&nbsp;directions,

00:10:04.877 --> 00:10:08.308
slowly letting the cables 
drop to the&nbsp;bottom of the ocean. &nbsp;

00:10:08.308 --> 00:10:12.014
A few days later, both ships&nbsp;
arrived at their destinations.

00:10:12.014 --> 00:10:13.680
 They had done it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:14.919
For the first time, 

00:10:14.919 --> 00:10:18.982
North America now had a 
direct&nbsp;communication link to Europe.

00:10:18.982 --> 00:10:20.671
 Information that&nbsp;would have taken weeks &nbsp;

00:10:20.671 --> 00:10:22.864
and an arduous journey by&nbsp;sea 

00:10:22.864 --> 00:10:25.760
could now be sent in just a matter of minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:27.515
Over the next 15 years

00:10:27.515 --> 00:10:30.844
undersea telegraph cables&nbsp;spread all over the world, 

00:10:30.844 --> 00:10:35.569
connecting Europe to&nbsp;India, Asia, 
Australia, and onto South America.&nbsp;

00:10:35.569 --> 00:10:37.632
In&nbsp;just a short period of time, 

00:10:37.632 --> 00:10:40.000
the world had changed&nbsp;forever

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.609
and it was all thanks to the incredible&nbsp;
engineers that learned to harness electricity.

